      ":question/id": "b322617c-ab21-5ce8-997f-2f10813b4280",
      ":question/math-academy-id": "e-5098",
      ":question/problem": "Plot the graph of the parametrically defined curve\n$x=t(t-6), \\,\\,\\, y=t^3, \\quad -2\\leq t \\leq 2$.",
      ":question/worked-solution": "We need to create a table of values and then sketch the curve. First, we work out the $x$-values using the formula $x=t(t-6)$. This gives\n\n| **$t$** | $-2$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $x$ | $16$ | $7$ | $0$ | $-5$ | $-8$ |\n| $y$ |  |  |  |  |  |\n\nNow, we work out the $y$ values using $y=t^3$. This gives\n\n| **$t$** | $-2$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $x$ | $16$ | $7$ | $0$ | $-5$ | $-8$ |\n| $y$ | $-8$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $8$ |\n\nPlotting these points, we obtain the following curve:\n\n![](image_assets/e492a940fa95b406b60c6b0a5c438d98-f15f25af0c.png)"
    },
